{"name":"Hornet Nest","hex":"#d5dfd3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hornet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any large wasp of the genus Vespa, having a brown-and-yellow-striped body and the ability to inflict a serious sting.","A person who pesters with petty but ceaseless attacks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hornet"},{"term":"nest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A structure built by a bird as a place to incubate eggs and rear young.","Any of various similar structures or sites used by other animals:\nA structure built, or site modified, to incubate eggs, rear young, or shelter a colony."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To build or settle into a nest.","To settle into a home."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hornet","blocks":[{"text":"Hornets are wasps of the genus Vespa in the subfamily Vespinae. They are the largest of the eusocial wasps. Species like the European hornet can grow to 35 mm (1.4 in) in length, while other species like the Asian giant hornet can exceed 50 mm (2.0 in). They are similar in appearance to their close relatives, the yellowjackets, but are distinguished from other vespine wasps by the relatively large top margin of the head, or vertex. Worldwide, 22 species of Vespa are recognized. Most species only occur in the tropics of Asia, though the European hornet is widely distributed throughout Europe, Russia, North America, and north-eastern Asia. Wasps of the genus Dolichovespula native to North America are commonly called hornets, but are actually yellowjackets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Hornet.jpg/330px-Hornet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornet","wikidata":"Q334422"},{"title":"Nest","blocks":[{"text":"A nest is a structure built by certain animals to hold their eggs or young. Although nests are most closely associated with birds, members of all classes of vertebrates and some invertebrates construct nests. They may be composed of organic material such as twigs, grass, and leaves, or may be a simple depression in the ground, or a hole in a rock, tree, or building. Human-made materials, such as string, plastic, cloth, or paper, may also be used. Nests can be found in all types of habitat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Yelagiri_bird%27s_nest.jpg/330px-Yelagiri_bird%27s_nest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nest","wikidata":"Q4785"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg/500px-Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg/1280px-Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg/500px-Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"European hornet with the remnants of a honey bee","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/%D0%96%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BE_%D1%88%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%88%D0%BD%D1%8F.jpg/500px-%D0%96%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BE_%D1%88%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%88%D0%BD%D1%8F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/%D0%96%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BE_%D1%88%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%88%D0%BD%D1%8F.jpg/1280px-%D0%96%D0